%[text] %[text:anchor:T_453a] # Estimate Bi-Directional CLLC Converter Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] This script helps you design and tune the CLLC converter resonant tank. It uses Control System Toolbox™ to plot the gain and impedance curves with the first harmonic approximation (FHA).
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1541] ## Estimate Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] You estimate the resonant tank parameters of the CLLC converter using the FHA based on a specified quality factor $\\left(Q\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}, inductance ratio$\\;\\left(k\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}, series resonant inductance factor$\\;\\left(a\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}, and resonant capacitance factor $\\left(b\\right)\\ldotp${"editStyle":"visual"} The inductance ratio is the ratio of magnetizing inductance $L\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} to primary series resonant inductance $L\_r${"editStyle":"visual"}. The series resonant inductance factor$\\;\\left(a\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} is the ratio of primary side referred secondary series resonant inductance to primary side series resonant inductor. The resonant capacitance factor$\\;\\left(b\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} is the ratio of primary side referred secondary resonant capacitance to primary side resonant capacitance. Soft switching depends on the magnetizing inductance. A higher magnetizing inductance lowers the magnetizing current and the conduction loss, but the primary switches need a minimum magnetizing current for zero-voltage switching.
%[text] The resonant tank parameters come from these equations:
%[text] $Q=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{L\_1 }{C\_1 }}}{R\_{\\mathrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} }${"editStyle":"visual"} $R\_{\\mathrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} =\\frac{8n^2 }{\\pi {\\;}^2 }R\_L${"editStyle":"visual"} $\\mathrm{transformer}\\;\\mathrm{turns}\\;\\mathrm{ratio},\\;n=\\frac{N\_1 }{N\_2 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] $f{\\;}\_1 =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{L\_1 C\_1 }}\\;${"editStyle":"visual"},   $f{\\;}\_2 =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{L\_2 C\_2 }}\\;${"editStyle":"visual"},  $k=\\frac{L\_m }{L\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"},  $a=\\frac{{n^2 L}\_2 }{L\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"},  $b=\\frac{C\_2 }{{n^2 C}\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text]  $f\_{\\textrm{r2}} =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\;\\sqrt{\\;\\left(L\_m +L\_1 \\right)C\_1 }}\\;${"editStyle":"visual"} is the lower resonant frequency at no load. As the load resistance tends to infinity, the secondary $L\_2 -C\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} arm carries no current, so the resonant loop reduces to $C\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}, $L\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}, and $L\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} in series.
%[text]  $x=\\frac{f\_s }{f\_r }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Soft switching needs two conditions at the same time. The peak magnetizing current must move the charge stored on the device output capacitances of one bridge leg pair within the dead time, and the tank must be inductive at the switching frequency. The transformer voltage is clamped for half a resonant period at or below resonance and for half a switching period above it, so the clamp frequency $f\_{\\textrm{clamp}} =\\max \\left(f\_r ,f\_s \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} sets the peak current $I\_{m-\\textrm{peak}} =V\_{\\textrm{in}} /\\left(4L\_m f\_{\\textrm{clamp}} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}, and the charge to move is $2C\_{\\textrm{parasitic}} V\_{\\textrm{in}}${"editStyle":"visual"}. The input voltage cancels, which leaves the charge condition
%[text] $T\_{\\textrm{dead}} \\ge 8\\;C\_{\\textrm{parasitic}} L\_m f\_{\\textrm{clamp}}${"editStyle":"visual"}, which bounds the magnetizing inductance at $L\_{m-\\max } =\\frac{T\_{\\textrm{dead}} }{8\\;C\_{\\textrm{parasitic}} f\_{\\textrm{clamp}} }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] The tank is inductive above the no-load lower resonance, so the phase condition is $f\_s \>f\_{\\textrm{r2}}${"editStyle":"visual"}. The highest switching frequency in service is the worst case, because the clamp frequency rises with it while the peak magnetizing current falls.
%[text] $R\_{\\textrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} =\\frac{8n^2 }{\\pi {\\;}^2 }\\left(\\frac{V\_o^2 }{P\_{\\textrm{load}} }\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] For $a=1\\;\\mathrm{and}\\;b=1,\\mathrm{the}\\;\\mathrm{resonant}\\;\\mathrm{frequencies}\\;f\_1 =f\_2${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Where,
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nQ-\\textrm{Quality}\\;\\textrm{factor}\\\\\nk-\\textrm{Inductance}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\na-\\mathrm{Series}\\;\\mathrm{resonant}\\;\\mathrm{inductance}\\;\\mathrm{factor}\\\\\nb-\\mathrm{Resonant}\\;\\mathrm{capacitance}\\;\\mathrm{factor}\\\\\nf\_1 -\\mathrm{Forward}\\;r\\textrm{esonant}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_2 -\\mathrm{Reverse}\\;r\\textrm{esonant}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_{\\textrm{r2}} -\\textrm{Second}\\;\\textrm{resonant}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_s -\\textrm{Switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nx-\\textrm{Frequency}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nL\_m -\\textrm{Magnetizing}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\\\\nL\_{m-\\max } -\\textrm{Maximum}\\;\\;m\\textrm{agnetizing}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\\\\nT\_{\\textrm{dead}} -\\textrm{Dead}\\;\\textrm{time}\\\\\nf\_{\\textrm{clamp}} -\\textrm{Clamp}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nI\_{m-\\textrm{peak}} -\\textrm{Peak}\\;\\textrm{magnetizing}\\;\\textrm{current}\\\\\nC\_{\\textrm{parasitic}} -\\textrm{Device}\\;\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{one}\\;\\textrm{switch}\\\\\nn-\\textrm{Transformer}\\;\\textrm{turns}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nR\_{\\textrm{ac}-\\textrm{effective}} -\\textrm{Effective}\\;\\textrm{AC}\\;\\textrm{load}\\;\\textrm{resistance}\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] ### Steps to calculate CLLC parameters
%[text] For a given quality factor $Q=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{L\_1 }{C\_1 }}}{R\_{\\textrm{eff}} }${"editStyle":"visual"} ,  and inductance ratio $k=\\frac{L\_m }{L\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Transformer turns ratio 
%[text] $\\;n=\\frac{V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\textrm{nominal}} }{V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\textrm{nominal}} }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Primary Capacitance C1
%[text] $C\_{\\;1} =\\frac{1}{\\omega {\\;}\_r R\_{\\textrm{eff}} Q}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Primary leakage inductance L1
%[text] $L\_1 =\\frac{1}{\\omega {\\;}\_r^2 C\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Magnetizing  inductance Lm
%[text] $L\_m ={\\textrm{kL}}\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Secondary leakage inductance L2
%[text] $L\_2 =\\frac{{\\textrm{aL}}\_1 }{n^2 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Secondary capacitance C2
%[text] $C\_2 ={bn^2 C}\_1${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_82a0] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Set your CLLC converter specifications.
CLLCSpec.minInputVoltage =  simscape.Value(380,"V"); % Minimum input voltage (V)
CLLCSpec.maxInputVoltage =  simscape.Value(400,"V"); % Maximum input voltage (V)
CLLCSpec.minOutputVoltage = simscape.Value(350,"V"); % Minimum output voltage (V)
CLLCSpec.maxOutputVoltage = simscape.Value(400,"V"); % Maximum output voltage (V)

CLLCSpec.ratedOutputPower = simscape.Value(10000,"W"); % Rated power (W)
CLLCSpec.resonantFrequency = simscape.Value(100e3,"Hz"); % Resonant frequency (Hz)
CLLCSpec.maxSwitchingFrequency = simscape.Value(170e3,"Hz"); % Highest switching frequency in service (Hz)
CLLCSpec.mosfetDrainSourceCapacitance = simscape.Value(200e-12,"F"); % Device output capacitance of one switch, as the charge-equivalent value Qoss/Vds (F)
nominalOutputVoltage = (CLLCSpec.minOutputVoltage.value+CLLCSpec.maxOutputVoltage.value)/2;
%[text] ### Specify Resonant Inductance and Capacitance Ratio
%[text] To get the same resonant frequency in the forward and reverse directions, set the resonant inductance and capacitance ratios close to unity.
CLLCSpec.a = 0.99; % Close to unity
CLLCSpec.b = 1.01; % Close to unity
%[text] To reach the required operating frequency range, adjust the quality factor and the inductance ratio using gain curves for various loads, quality factors, and inductance ratios.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_mag] ## Specify Magnetizing Current Limit
%[text] The magnetizing current must be large enough to charge and discharge the device output capacitances within the dead time for zero-voltage switching, but small enough that its reactive current does not dominate the conduction loss. Specify the voltage pair that sets the transformer turns ratio, or set the ratio directly, and set the allowed peak magnetizing current as a percentage of the rated current of the port that sources power at the resonant frequency.
%[text] Because the CLLC converter is bidirectional, `computeCLLCMagnetizingCurrentLimit` evaluates both power-flow directions and reports the worse of the two. In both directions the rated current of the sourcing port, referred to the primary, is
%[text] $I\_{\\textrm{rated}} =\\frac{P}{V\_{L\_m } }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] where $P${"editStyle":"visual"} is the rated power and $V\_{L\_m }${"editStyle":"visual"} is the primary-referred voltage that drives the magnetizing inductance. In the forward direction the primary bridge drives it, so $V\_{L\_m } =V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\textrm{avg}}${"editStyle":"visual"}. In the reverse direction the secondary bridge drives it, so $V\_{L\_m } =n\\;V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\textrm{avg}}${"editStyle":"visual"}, with $n${"editStyle":"visual"} the realized turns ratio. The two limits therefore differ only by the integer-winding rounding of the turns ratio, and the resulting $I\_m /I\_{m-\\textrm{limit}}${"editStyle":"visual"} ratios differ by the *square* of that rounding.
%[text] The average voltage pair centers the gain requirement in both directions. Either extreme pair biases the turns ratio towards one end of the voltage window and favors one direction over the other. The average pair also minimizes the magnetizing current: the forward requirement on $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} falls as $1/n^2${"editStyle":"visual"} while the reverse requirement rises as $n^2${"editStyle":"visual"}, so the worse of the two is smallest exactly where they cross, at $n=V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\textrm{avg}} /V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\textrm{avg}}${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%[text] The secondary winding count anchors the integer winding pair. Choose it so that $N\_1 =\\textrm{round}\\left(n\\times N\_2 \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} lands on, or very close to, the requested ratio. An exact pair makes the forward and reverse magnetizing currents identical. The ratio set below is $n=0\\ldotp 9975${"editStyle":"visual"}, and $N\_2 =29\\to N\_1 =29${"editStyle":"visual"}, which realizes a ratio of 1. Use the same value in every function call below, otherwise the swept tank and the designed tank describe different transformers.
%[text] To set the turns ratio, set defineTurnsRatio. A lower ratio raises the gain the tank must supply and a higher one lowers it; an empty value derives the ratio from the voltage pair above. To plot one gain curve per ratio, give `plotGainCurveCLLCConverter` a vector of ratios. The required gain range comes from the voltage specification, so it does not move with the ratio.
  %[control:button:1a7c]{"position":[1,2]}
CLLCSpec.turnratioVoltageLevel = "Max Output Voltage and Min Input Voltage"; % Which voltage pair anchors the turns ratio %[control:dropdown:2c1a]{"position":[34,76]}
defineTurnsRatio = []; % Turns ratio N1/N2. If empty, derived from the voltage specification (turnratioVoltageLevel)

CLLCSpec.magCurrentPercentofInputCurrent = 70; % Peak magnetizing current limit, as a percentage of the rated current of the sourcing port at the resonant frequency
CLLCSpec.minimumAllowedDeadTime = simscape.Value(200e-9,"s"); % Minimum dead time the processor and switching devices allow
CLLCSpec.maximumAllowedDeadTime = simscape.Value(0.08/CLLCSpec.resonantFrequency.value,"s"); % Maximum dead time, typically 5 to 10 % of a PWM period
secondaryWindingTurns = 29; % Secondary winding turns, the anchor for the integer winding pair
computeCLLCMagnetizingCurrentLimit(CLLCSpec,N2=secondaryWindingTurns,TurnsRatio=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:336087ce]
%%
%[text] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
CLLCSpec.Rco = 0.02; % Output filter capacitor ESR (Ohm)
CLLCSpec.R1 = 1e-2; % Transformer primary resistance (Ohm)
CLLCSpec.R2 = 1e-2; % Transformer secondary resistance (Ohm)
CLLCSpec.Rcr1 = 1e-3; % Primary resonant capacitor resistance (Ohm)
CLLCSpec.Rcr2 = 1e-3; % Secondary resonant capacitor resistance (Ohm)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6293] ## Plot Gain Curve of the Resonant Tank
%[text] To estimate the forward and reverse gain curves of the resonant tank with the FHA, this design solution uses Control System Toolbox and the `GainCurveCLLCConverterForward` and `GainCurveCLLCConverterReverse` models.
%[text] You can plot the gain curves for various quality factors, inductance ratios, and loads, together with the minimum and maximum gain that your voltage specification requires. The summary reports the minimum and maximum operating frequency that achieve the required gain range.
%[text] When you sweep the quality factor and the inductance ratio in the following subsections, check that:
%[text] - The product $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} exceeds the minimum value reported by `computeCLLCMagnetizingCurrentLimit`. The summary table reports $I\_m /I\_{m-\\textrm{limit}}${"editStyle":"visual"} for every design point in both directions, so you can read the margin directly instead of inferring it.
%[text] - The gain curve peak at full load rises at least 5 to 10 % above the maximum gain line, in both directions. This gives the controller enough slope to regulate without large frequency swings.
%[text] - The maximum switching frequency at full load stays below about 1.5 to 1.6 times the resonant frequency, to limit core and switching losses.
%[text] - All criteria hold at both full load and light load. Full load is the hardest case for the peak margin, light load for the frequency range. \
%[text] Raising $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} lowers the magnetizing current and with it the conduction loss, but it also flattens the gain curve and lowers its peak, which shrinks the control margin. The two requirements conflict, so expect a compromise. A minimum or maximum frequency reported as `NaN` means the requested gain range is not reachable at that operating point: `NaN` for the minimum frequency means the peak never reaches the maximum required gain, and `NaN` for the maximum frequency means the curve never falls to the minimum required gain within the swept frequency range.
%[text] To open the gain curve models, at the MATLAB Command Window, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] open_system("GainCurveCLLCConverterForward"); % Gain curve for forward direction
%[text] open_system("GainCurveCLLCConverterReverse"); % Gain curve for reverse direction
%[text] ```
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3d2a] ### Different DC Load Resistance
%[text] Estimate the resonant tank parameters for the given quality factor and inductance ratio, then plot the gain curve for various loads.  
  %[control:button:084c]{"position":[1,2]}
qualityFactor = 0.4; % Quality factor
k = 13; % Inductance ratio

outputLoadPU = [0.4 0.6 1]; % DC output load (pu)
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(outputLoadPU*CLLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm"); % Output DC resistance

gainCurve = plotGainCurveCLLCConverter(CLLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:35eb736f] %[output:70ac6853] %[output:0a51edd7]
    LmLrRatioVec=k,RloadVec=Rload,N2=secondaryWindingTurns,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %#ok<*NASGU> %[output:group:35eb736f] %[output:70ac6853] %[output:0a51edd7]
%[text] To hold the output voltage over the input voltage range, the gain curves must cover the required gain range at every load. If the resonant tank does not meet the criteria, adjust the quality factor and the inductance ratio.
%[text] The load sweep is the most informative one for a CLLC converter, because the quality factor scales with the load: $Q\_{\\textrm{eff}} =Q\_{\\textrm{rated}} \\times P/P\_{\\textrm{rated}}${"editStyle":"visual"}. The inductance ratio is fixed by the tank; only the quality factor moves. A CLLC gain curve is single-lobed with a tall peak while $Q\_{\\textrm{eff}} \\sqrt{k}${"editStyle":"visual"} is small and splits into two lobes with a much lower usable peak once it is large, so light load is the easy case for gain and full load is the hard one, the opposite of an LLC converter. From light to full load, the peak collapses.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4efc] ### Different Quality Factor
%[text] To reach the required gain and operating frequency range, plot the gain curve for various quality factors at a given inductance ratio and load, then select the quality factor that suits your application.
  %[control:button:57bd]{"position":[1,2]}
qualityFactor = [0.2 0.4 0.55]; % Quality factor
k = 13; % Inductance ratio
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(CLLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm");
gainCurve = plotGainCurveCLLCConverter(CLLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:09c15dc5] %[output:4f92879b] %[output:6a01710a]
    LmLrRatioVec=k,RloadVec=Rload,N2=secondaryWindingTurns,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:group:09c15dc5] %[output:4f92879b] %[output:6a01710a]
%[text] Watch the $I\_m /I\_{m-\\textrm{limit}}${"editStyle":"visual"} column as well as the frequency range. The magnetizing current limit sets a *floor* on $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"}, so the lowest quality factor in this sweep is the one that violates it, not the highest.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7667] ### Different Inductance Ratio
%[text] To further improve the gain and operating range, keep the quality factor from the previous step, vary the inductance ratio at a given load resistance, and select the inductance ratio from the gain curves.
  %[control:button:6400]{"position":[1,2]}
qualityFactor = 0.23; % Quality factor
k = [10 13 20]; % Inductance ratio vector
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)/CLLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value,"Ohm");
gainCurve = plotGainCurveCLLCConverter(CLLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:21dcfa40] %[output:2ca8f364] %[output:98f1f82c]
    LmLrRatioVec=k,RloadVec=Rload,N2=secondaryWindingTurns,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:group:21dcfa40] %[output:2ca8f364] %[output:98f1f82c]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_89d4] ## The Selected Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] For the given quality factor and inductance ratio, to estimate the CLLC converter resonant tank parameters that meet your requirements, use the `designCLLCFullBridgeConverter` function.
  %[control:button:3672]{"position":[1,2]}
CLLCSpec.qualityFactor = 0.19; % Quality factor %[output:942c0db6]
CLLCSpec.k = 15; % Inductance ratio
%[text] The two load points below bracket the coverage boundary explained in the next section: at 55 % of rated power the gain curve is still single-lobed and covers the whole required window in both directions, while at rated power it has split and the minimum frequency is reported as `NaN` because the peak no longer reaches the maximum required gain.
defineTurnsRatio = 1.03;
selectedLoadPU = [0.7 1]; % DC output load (pu)
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./(selectedLoadPU*CLLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm");
[~, Frequency, frequencyRange] = plotGainCurveCLLCConverter(CLLCSpec,QualityFactorVec=CLLCSpec.qualityFactor, ... %[output:group:5ffb0edd] %[output:44b70fe0] %[output:64301166]
    LmLrRatioVec=CLLCSpec.k,RloadVec=Rload,N2=secondaryWindingTurns,TurnsRatioVec=defineTurnsRatio); %[output:group:5ffb0edd] %[output:44b70fe0] %[output:64301166]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_500a] ## Estimate CLLC Converter Parameters
%[text] To estimate the CLLC converter parameters from your specifications, quality factor, and inductance ratio, use the `designCLLCFullBridgeConverter` function.
[designTable, CLLCDesign]  = designCLLCFullBridgeConverter(CLLCSpec,N2=secondaryWindingTurns,TurnsRatio=defineTurnsRatio); % Estimate CLLC converter parameters %[output:8169bd51]
%[text] 
%[text] Verify that the selected parameters provide soft switching over the required frequency range. Use the `estimateCLLCMaximumMagnetizingInductance` function to get the maximum magnetizing inductance, the dead time the selected inductance needs, and the no-load lower resonance. Check the lowest and the highest switching frequency in service. The highest frequency is the worst case, because the clamp frequency is $\\max \\left(f\_r ,f\_s \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} and the peak magnetizing current falls as that frequency rises.
magL = estimateCLLCMaximumMagnetizingInductance(CLLCDesign,SwitchingFrequency=simscape.Value(60e3,"Hz"),DisplayTableFlag=true); % Lowest switching frequency in service %[output:84baf11a]
magL = estimateCLLCMaximumMagnetizingInductance(CLLCDesign,SwitchingFrequency=CLLCSpec.maxSwitchingFrequency,DisplayTableFlag=true); % Highest switching frequency in service %[output:7fa888a2]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_feasibility] ## Gain Ratio and Design Feasibility
%[text] The **gain ratio** of a specification is the ratio of the maximum required gain to the minimum required gain:
%[text] $\\textrm{gain}\\textrm{\_}\\textrm{ratio}=\\frac{V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\max } \\times V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\max } }{V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\min } \\times V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\min } }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] This ratio is fixed by the voltage specification and is independent of the turns ratio $n${"editStyle":"visual"}. For a bidirectional converter it is also the same in both directions: the forward gain range spans $V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\max } /V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\min }${"editStyle":"visual"} down to $V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\min } /V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\max }${"editStyle":"visual"}, and the reverse range spans $V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\max } /V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\min }${"editStyle":"visual"} down to $V\_{\\textrm{in}-\\min } /V\_{\\textrm{out}-\\max }${"editStyle":"visual"}, and both ratios reduce to the same expression.
%[text] The gain ratio tells you how much dynamic range the tank must cover, but on its own it is a weak feasibility screen for a bidirectional CLLC converter. Two constraints decide feasibility, and they act on $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} from opposite sides.
%[text] **The magnetizing current limit sets a floor.** Substituting the peak magnetizing current into $k\\times Q=\\omega\_1 L\_m /R\_{\\textrm{eff}}${"editStyle":"visual"} eliminates $L\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} entirely, and for an exact integer winding pair the requirement collapses to
%[text] $k\\times Q\\ge \\frac{\\pi^3 }{16\\left(I\_m \\right.}${"editStyle":"visual"},   that is,   $I\_m${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] There is no voltage, power or frequency in that expression, so no change of specification relaxes the magnetizing current constraint. At $I\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} the floor is $k\\times Q\\ge 4\\ldotp 845${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%[text] **The gain curve split sets a ceiling, and it is a power level, not a tank property.** Because $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} is defined against the effective load resistance, $Q\_{\\textrm{eff}} =Q\_{\\textrm{rated}} \\times P/P\_{\\textrm{rated}}${"editStyle":"visual"}, so the inductance ratio is fixed by the tank but the quality factor rises with load. Beyond roughly $Q\_{\\textrm{eff}} \\sqrt{k}\\approx 0\\ldotp 79${"editStyle":"visual"} the curve splits into two lobes and the usable peak gain collapses to only a few percent above unity, so the full voltage window is available only up to
%[text] $\\frac{P\_{\\max -\\textrm{full}\\;\\textrm{window}} }{P\_{\\textrm{rated}} }\\approx \\frac{0\\ldotp 79}{Q\_{\\textrm{rated}} \\sqrt{k}}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] The invariant that governs the split is $Q\\sqrt{k}${"editStyle":"visual"}, not $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"}. Physically $Q\\sqrt{k}=\\sqrt{L\_m /C\_1 }/R\_{\\textrm{eff}}${"editStyle":"visual"} is the quality factor of the $f\_{r2}${"editStyle":"visual"} loop itself, so the split is a question of whether the load damps the low-frequency resonance enough to merge it with the $f\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} lobe. Measured by bisecting $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} at each $k${"editStyle":"visual"} from 8 to 60, $Q\\sqrt{k}${"editStyle":"visual"} at the boundary holds to within 10 % of 0.79 while $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} spreads from 2.37 to 5.90, an 88 % spread. Anything derived from a constant $k\\times Q${"editStyle":"visual"} boundary is only valid at the inductance ratio it was calibrated at.
%[text] Combining the floor and the ceiling, and substituting $k\\times Q\_{\\textrm{rated}} =193\\ldotp 79/I\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} from the floor, gives the law that governs a symmetric bidirectional CLLC design:
%[text] $\\frac{P\_{\\max -\\textrm{full}\\;\\textrm{window}} }{P\_{\\textrm{rated}} }\\approx \\frac{\\sqrt{k}\\;I\_m }{}${"editStyle":"visual"},   so the full window at *full* power needs   $k\\ge \\left(\\frac{245\\ldotp 3}{I\_m }\\right.${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] A tight magnetizing current limit and a full voltage window at full power can coexist, but they force a large inductance ratio: $I\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} needs $k\\ge 38${"editStyle":"visual"} with $Q\_{\\textrm{rated}} =0\\ldotp 128${"editStyle":"visual"}, while $I\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} of 55, 68 and 85 % need $k\\ge 20${"editStyle":"visual"}, 13 and 8.3 respectively. What rules the large-$k${"editStyle":"visual"} branch out in practice is not the magnetizing current but the frequency span: $f\_{r2} =f\_1 /\\sqrt{k+1}${"editStyle":"visual"} falls to about 15 kHz at $k=44${"editStyle":"visual"}, and the buck edge at light load runs past 1 MHz, a span of more than 17 times.
%[text] The practical choice is therefore a moderate inductance ratio that accepts the full window only up to part load. That usually suits a battery charger, because the maximum gain point (battery full, constant voltage, tapering current) occurs at low power, where the curve is single-lobed and tall, while the minimum gain point (constant current, full current) occurs at high power, where the curve is low and easy to buck. Check the design against the real charge and discharge profile rather than against a single window. If it still does not fit, narrow the voltage window, raise the magnetizing current percentage, move along the floor to larger $k${"editStyle":"visual"} and lower $Q${"editStyle":"visual"} and accept the wider frequency span, or accept burst operation at light load.
gainRatio = (CLLCSpec.maxOutputVoltage.value * CLLCSpec.maxInputVoltage.value) / ...
    (CLLCSpec.minOutputVoltage.value * CLLCSpec.minInputVoltage.value);
fprintf("Gain ratio = %.3f (identical in the forward and reverse directions)\n", gainRatio); %[output:7d2d2065]
%[text] ## Plot the Impedance Curve
%[text] You can plot the impedance curve of the resonant tank in the low-frequency capacitive region and the high-frequency inductive region.
  %[control:button:9810]{"position":[1,2]}
Rload = simscape.Value((nominalOutputVoltage^2)./([0.4 0.6 1]*CLLCSpec.ratedOutputPower.value),"Ohm");
impedanceData = plotCLLCResonantTankImpedanceCurve(CLLCDesign,RloadVec=Rload); %[output:4b4eefaa] %[output:38665fb3]
%%
